 "posts": [
  {
   "slug": "what-a-pressure-test-teaches-you-in-the-first-month",
   "title": "What a pressure test teaches you in the first month",
   "date": "2026-11-12",
   "categories": [
    "bench-notes"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "pressure-testing",
    "year-one"
   ],
   "image": "assets/images/story-farid.webp",
   "excerpt": "Emeka Obi on why the first pressure test of a new apprentice almost always leaks, and why that is the point.",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A pressure test is the moment a joint stops being an opinion. You close the valves, bring the pipework up to pressure and watch a gauge. If the needle holds, the joints are good. If it creeps down, something leaks, and the only way to find it is to look.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In the first month nearly every apprentice’s piece leaks somewhere. That is not a failure; it is the lesson. We ask you to find the leak yourself with a dry tissue and a steady hand, to cut out the joint, clean it and make it again. Most people find that the second joint is better than the first four.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>By the end of the month the gauge has become less frightening. You learn to read it, to wait for it and to trust it. That habit, checking before you say something works, is the most useful thing the bench teaches, and it applies to every job you will go on to do.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n"
  }
 ],
